      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Critics of federal reparations for the descendants of enslaved people often argue that the U.S. Constitution's 13th and 14th Amendments already morally and legally mandated reparations through the end of slavery and, in the case of the 14th, by guaranteeing \"just compensation\" for property (slaves) taken by the government and \"equal protection of the laws.\"\n\nFurthermore, opponents frequently emphasize the procedural and financial improprieties of not just the time period when the 13th and 14th amendments were ratified but also the fact that the 13th Amendment explicitly \"abolish or abolish slavery\" without any notion of \"just compensation\" for the individual \"property\" of the enslaved.\n\nFinally, some argue that societal inequality is the result of systemic and ongoing misdeeds rather than specific individual wrongful acts sufficient to warrant a bill of \"just compensation\" for any single person of questionable claims of \"property\" claim against the government.",
