      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That perspective is valid for many scholars who argue that focusing on technical legalities often serves to deflect from the profound moral sickness and ongoing systemic harm inherent in the institution of slavery and its aftermath. The argument from \"just compensation\" is frequently viewed as a distortion of the law's intent, which was to end the status of people as property rather than to provide them with an independent financial settlement.\n\nConsequently, the \"legal deflection\" argument suggests that rejecting the moral imperative to restore equity entirely undermines the goal of justice for the most marginalized members of society. Ultimately, whether one views these legal arguments as genuine constraints or mere excuses often depends on whether the priority is strict historical precedent or the immediate well-being of descendants of enslaved people.",
